::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A survey records favorite school clubs: art, robotics, debate, and music. Which display should compare the number of students in each club?
{
=A. bar graph#Correct. A bar graph is best for comparing counts across categories.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A bar graph shows 31 students prefer videos and 18 prefer printed notes. Which statement is supported?
{
=A. More students prefer videos than printed notes.#Correct. The video bar is larger, so that comparison is supported.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A nurse records patient wait times in minutes for one day. Which display best shows how the wait times are distributed by intervals?
{
=A. histogram#Correct. A histogram groups numerical data into intervals.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A line graph shows weekly savings increasing from January through April. What does the graph show?
{
=A. Savings increased over time.#Correct. An upward line across months shows growth over time.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A table sorts students by grade level and lunch choice. Which display organizes both categorical variables?
{
=A. two-way table#Correct. A two-way table organizes two categorical variables at the same time.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A circle graph shows 40% of a budget is rent. What does 40% represent?
{
=A. rent is 40 parts out of every 100 parts of the budget#Correct. Percent means part per 100, so 40% describes the rent share.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A manager wants to show sales for each month across a year. Which display emphasizes change over time?
{
=A. line graph#Correct. A line graph is designed to show change over ordered time periods.
~B. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A histogram has the tallest bar for 20-29 minutes. What is supported?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. The 20-29 minute interval has the greatest frequency.#Correct. The tallest histogram bar represents the interval with the most data values.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which data set is categorical?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. favorite type of movie#Correct. Movie type is a label, so it is categorical.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A display has no title or axis labels. What is the main problem?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. The reader cannot tell what the data represent.#Correct. Labels and titles are needed to interpret the context correctly.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which data set is numerical?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. number of minutes spent reading#Correct. Minutes are measured with numbers, so the data are numerical.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A bar graph shows 12 students chose art and 20 chose music. How many more chose music?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. 8#Correct. Subtract the category counts: 20 - 12 = 8.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which display should be used for counts of phone brands owned by students?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
=B. bar graph#Correct. Phone brands are categories, and bar graphs compare category counts.
~C. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A relative frequency table shows 25% chose walking. What does this mean?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. One fourth of the group chose walking.#Correct. 25% is equivalent to one fourth of the total group.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A scientist records temperatures every hour. Which display can show the pattern through the day?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. line graph#Correct. Hourly temperatures are ordered by time, so a line graph shows the pattern.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A two-way table compares grade level and internet access. What kind of variables are shown?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. two categorical variables#Correct. Grade level and access category are labels, so both are categorical.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which display would be least appropriate for comparing four named categories?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. histogram#Correct. A histogram is for numerical intervals, not named categories.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A graph scale jumps from 0 to 100 to 105 to 110. Why might this be misleading?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. The uneven scale can exaggerate differences.#Correct. Uneven scales can make small differences look larger than they are.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A display groups ages as 10-19, 20-29, and 30-39. What display type is this most likely?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
=C. histogram#Correct. Age is numerical, and the values are grouped into intervals.
~D. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A bar graph shows equal bars for bus and car. What conclusion is supported?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. The same number of students chose bus and car.#Correct. Equal bar heights represent equal frequencies.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which display is best when the goal is to show parts of a whole budget?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. circle graph#Correct. A circle graph can show how each category makes up part of a whole.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A data display shows sample results from only 5 students. What caution is reasonable?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. The sample may be too small to represent all students.#Correct. A very small sample may not represent the larger group well.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
Which choice should come first when selecting a display?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. identify whether the data are categorical or numerical#Correct. The display must match the data type and question.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.2
Question:
A line graph decreases each week. Which phrase describes the trend?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. downward trend#Correct. A decreasing line over time is a downward trend.
}
::MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U03_L01_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.DL.1
Question:
A survey response is yes or no. What type of data is this?
{
~A. scatterplot#This choice does not match the data type or purpose in the question.
~B. random list#A random list does not organize the data clearly for interpretation.
~C. unlabeled graph#A display without labels cannot communicate the data meaning reliably.
=D. categorical data#Correct. Yes and no are categories, not measured numbers.
}
